Quick Sort (Divide-and-Conquer)
-------------------------------
Algorithmic Execution Workflow
 Step 1 (Pivot Selection): Consider either the first, last, or any random number as the pivot
element.
 Step 2 (Pointer Initialization): Initialize two pointers: the first element acts as pointer 'p';
and the last element acts as pointer 'q'.
 Step 3 (Scanning Loop): Pointer 'p' moves in the forward direction and checks for elements
where p > pivot. Pointer 'q' moves in the backward direction and checks for elements where
q &lt; pivot.
 Step 4 (Conditionals &amp; Partitioning): If 'p' and 'q' have not crossed each other, swap the
elements at 'p' and 'q'. If 'p' and 'q' have crossed, swap the pivot element with the element at
'q'.

Dry Run Trace Example
---------------------
Given initial array: [30, 45, 12, 22, 60, 20, 96, 35]
 Let Pivot = 30. Initial indices: p points to 45, q points to 35.
 p scans forward for &gt; 30 (stops at 45); q scans backward for < 30 (stops at 20).
 Since p and q are not crossed, swap(p, q) -> Array becomes: [30, 20, 12, 22, 60, 45, 96, 35]
 Resume scanning: p stops at 60; q stops at 22. Now p and q have crossed.
 Swap pivot (30) with q (22) -> Sub-partitioned Array: [22, 20, 12] 30 [60, 45, 96, 35]
 Recursively sorting Left sub-array [22, 20, 12] yields [12, 20, 22].
 Recursively sorting Right sub-array [60, 45, 96, 35] yields [35, 45, 60, 96].
Result: Combined Sorted Order = Left + Middle + Right = [12, 20, 22, 30, 35, 45, 60, 96]

Code
-----
def quickSort(arr):
    if len(arr)<=1:
        return arr
    pivot=arr[len(arr)//2]
    left=[x for x in arr if x<pivot]
    middle=[x for x in arr if x==pivot]
    right=[x for x in arr if x>pivot]
    return quickSort(left)+middle+quickSort(right)
    
a=quickSort([30,45,12,22,60,20,96,35])
print(a)

Output
------
[12, 20, 22, 30, 35, 45, 60, 96]
